  "body": "### What are you trying to do\n\nCounterbores for M6 cap screws in aluminium: Ø11 × 7 deep, cut with a Ø6 flat end mill on a small 3-axis mill (LinuxCNC, ER20 spindle). The usual way to cut these is helical interpolation: the tool spirals down on the wall radius to full depth, then runs one flat circle at the floor. It never plunges straight into solid material.\n\nIn 0.24.0 the Bore operation is a level-by-level ring ladder. At each step-down level the tool dives at the hole centre, sweeps out to the wall and runs a ring, then moves to the next level. The source describes the first engagement as the bore's centre dive, and the Lead-in tab has no entry choice for Bore. Linking → Level to level → Helix helps between levels, but that helix runs on a 1 mm radius at the centre and the first dive is still a plunge. With a 1 mm step-down that comes to 7 plunges and 7 rings per counterbore.\n\nOn a light machine, a flat end mill plunging into solid aluminium is the move most likely to chatter, pack chips or snap the tool. That's why the helical cycle is the default for counterbores and holes bored with an end mill.\n\n### How you imagine it working\n\nA **Helical** strategy in the Bore operation (or a separate Helical bore operation):\n\n- The tool positions over the hole at the retract plane and descends on a **continuous helix** whose radius puts the cutter's rim on the finished wall (hole radius − tool radius − stock to leave), from Top Z to the hole floor.\n- **Helix pitch** (mm per turn) is set by the user, with a sensible automatic value. Alternatively a maximum ramp angle.\n- At the floor, **one full flat circle** to remove the ramp step, then a move off the wall towards the centre before retracting.\n- Optional **spring pass**: a second floor circle with no extra depth.\n- **Climb or conventional**, as Bore has now.\n- For holes too wide for one helix to clear the core (wider than about 2 × tool diameter): helix at an inner radius first, then step out in rings at the floor, or simply refuse with a clear message.\n- **Through holes:** continue the helix past the floor by the break-through allowance.\n\nThe rest of Bore can stay as it is (hole detection, per-hole floors, the reach checks).\n\nHappy to test builds on the counterbore case above and air-cut the result on my machine.\n",
